1
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:40,580
We Need a Horse

2
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:15,990
Come on, you bastard!

3
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,620
Einar! Einar, stop.

4
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:25,370
Let's dig it out a little more
and cut the roots.

5
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:27,640
We won't get it out like this.

6
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,010
Piece of shit!

7
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:36,690
It still won't budge.

8
00:02:47,010 --> 00:02:48,770
Come on!

9
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:03,010
We need a horse. We won't get
anywhere without a horse.

10
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:10,220
They lent us one to move the lumber,
so why don't we have one to prepare the soil?

11
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:11,860
It doesn't make sense.

12
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,560
They didn't lend us a horse.

13
00:03:15,050 --> 00:03:18,510
The retainers just made us
do their job for them.

14
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:21,790
What do you want to do, Einar?

15
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:25,270
Should we save preparing the fields for
later and chop down more trees first?

16
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:31,250
No, I'll try talking to them again.

17
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:33,050
It might be a waste of time, though.

18
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:37,030
You guys don't know when to quit.

19
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:39,240
No means no.

20
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:43,540
But we have Pater's permission.

21
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:45,440
Pater?

22
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:47,920
You mean the former slave?

23
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:49,590
In any case, no.

24
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:52,170
We're using all the horses for other work.

25
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:54,230
We don't have any to spare for you.

26
00:03:57,170 --> 00:04:00,600
Come on, slaves. You're wasting your time.

27
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,240
Quit slacking off and
head back to your woods.

28
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,370
Hello.

29
00:04:05,870 --> 00:04:09,930
What's this? I only heard one "hello."

30
00:04:09,930 --> 00:04:13,420
I see the big lad is as rude as ever.

31
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:16,680
Hey, we're taking a horse.

32
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:17,780
Sure.

33
00:04:21,310 --> 00:04:26,660
Now that I think about it,
you slaves are no different from livestock.

34
00:04:27,810 --> 00:04:33,040
Just borrowing a plot of land and
running a farm is insolent enough.

35
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:38,630
Use your own bodies to do the work.

36
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:47,230
That jerk. Our only choice
is to talk to the master.

37
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:49,180
We need a horse.

38
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:52,130
Not just for removing stumps,
but for tilling the soil, too.

39
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:55,910
I'm not gonna let their bullying stop me.

40
00:04:56,900 --> 00:05:01,180
But even if the master's influence
allows us to borrow a horse,

41
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,430
we'd just be upsetting the retainers.

42
00:05:03,930 --> 00:05:06,040
They'll hate us even more.

43
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:10,150
What are we gonna do, then?
Do all the work ourselves?

44
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:14,050
Who knows how many years it'll
take for us to finally start farming?

45
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:16,780
The woods are vast.

46
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:20,440
Couldn't we wait to start farming
until we've cleared more land?

47
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,490
It's impossible for us to level
the land and start growing crops.

48
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,030
Thorfinn!

49
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:32,970
Why are you so unmotivated?

50
00:05:32,970 --> 00:05:34,810
Don't you want to be free?

51
00:05:35,230 --> 00:05:37,980
We're gonna grow crops, no matter what.

52
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:41,990
Then we'll sell them to the master
and buy back our freedom.

53
00:05:41,990 --> 00:05:45,590
And to do that, we need a horse!

54
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:52,350
For a guy who never eats a square meal,
you've sure got a lot of energy.

55
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:55,800
You just don't have enough energy, asshole!

56
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:59,510
I think I get the picture.

57
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:09,920
Hup, and... Ow, ow, ow...

58
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:18,520
What are you doing? Over here. Come on.

59
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,170
Can't have a conversation
standing this far apart.

60
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:24,930
Don't step on the ridges.

61
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:35,910
Hello. Um, can we help you?

62
00:06:35,910 --> 00:06:37,320
What are your names?

63
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:39,850
Oh, right. I'm Einar.

64
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:41,000
I'm Thorfinn.

65
00:06:41,610 --> 00:06:42,970
Shall I lend you one?

66
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:43,750
What?

67
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:47,430
A horse. You need a horse, don't you?

68
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:49,270
What?

69
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:54,120
Y-Yes, we need a horse!

70
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,270
Wait, for real?

71
00:06:56,270 --> 00:07:00,390
That'd be a huge help.

72
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:02,820
Uh, actually...

73
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:07,510
I'm sorry, but who are you?

74
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:23,840
My back is killing me.

75
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:35,350
Uh, um... Gramps?

76
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:37,450
It's Sverkel.

77
00:07:37,450 --> 00:07:39,610
Mister Sverkel to you.

78
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:44,740
Mister Sverkel, how long do we have
to keep picking out these rocks?

79
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:47,490
Until the rocks are gone.

80
00:07:47,490 --> 00:07:49,980
Come on. I don't see your hands moving.

81
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:51,550
Oh, s-sorry.

82
00:07:53,990 --> 00:07:55,000
Ow...

83
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,230
Ow...

84
00:07:56,230 --> 00:07:57,880
Good.

85
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:01,500
So, um, Mister Sverkel!

86
00:08:01,830 --> 00:08:05,070
About you lending us a horse...

87
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:06,680
Carry the basket, would you?

88
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:17,190
Once you're done chopping firewood,
draw up some water.

89
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:18,280
Be quick.

90
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:19,510
Yes, sir.

91
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:27,670
He's totally taking advantage of us.

92
00:08:27,670 --> 00:08:29,950
We don't have time for this.

93
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:35,450
Does that old man live
in this house all alone?

94
00:08:37,430 --> 00:08:40,830
Who knows? I've never met him before, either.

95
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:44,390
Is he really going to lend us a horse?

96
00:08:44,390 --> 00:08:49,030
What if he's just making us
work for him for free?

97
00:08:49,910 --> 00:08:52,840
Either way, this is a typical day for us.

98
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:55,330
After all, we're slaves.

99
00:08:55,850 --> 00:08:58,080
We're the master's slaves.

100
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:01,730
I don't care what your circumstances are.

101
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:03,950
This is our agreement.

102
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:07,990
In exchange for borrowing a horse,
you'll do chores at my house

103
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:11,740
when you pick it up and when you drop it off.

104
00:09:12,290 --> 00:09:13,890
Got it?

105
00:09:24,030 --> 00:09:27,130
All right. Let's get the next one.

106
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:29,530
Pull!

107
00:09:30,510 --> 00:09:32,390
Boy, that was easy.

108
00:09:32,390 --> 00:09:33,890
I feel amazing.

109
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:36,940
I can't believe he actually
let us borrow a horse.

110
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:38,890
That old man is a good guy.

111
00:09:45,080 --> 00:09:46,770
Hup!

112
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:57,290
Come on, you can do it.

113
00:09:57,290 --> 00:09:58,630
That's it. Just like that.

114
00:09:58,630 --> 00:10:00,050
You're such a cute horsey.

115
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:04,570
All right.

116
00:10:04,570 --> 00:10:06,820
Next stump.

117
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:48,070
At this rate, we might actually be ready
in time for sowing season in the fall.

118
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:54,170
Maybe the old man will even lend us a plow.

119
00:10:54,170 --> 00:10:57,160
I hope he'll lend us a heavy one with wheels.

120
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:00,220
What do we plant in fall?

121
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:01,220
What?

122
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:05,620
Wheat, obviously.

123
00:11:06,210 --> 00:11:09,730
Really? You plant wheat in the fall, huh?

124
00:11:10,790 --> 00:11:12,480
What?

125
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:18,040
Hey, hey, wait a minute.
Have you ever worked on a farm before?

126
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:19,240
No.

127
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:23,430
Nobody in my homeland farmed.

128
00:11:24,110 --> 00:11:27,090
And since I was a kid,
I worked on the battlefield.

129
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:30,500
A land without farms...

130
00:11:31,990 --> 00:11:33,650
I don't believe it.

131
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:41,990
My mom and sister
would be shocked to hear that.

132
00:11:46,390 --> 00:11:50,190
Well, don't worry.
Leave all the farming stuff to me.

133
00:11:50,190 --> 00:11:52,750
After all, I'm a professional.

134
00:11:52,750 --> 00:11:53,570
Sure.

135
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:56,150
Hey, slaves.

136
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:00,660
Who gave you permission to take that horse?

137
00:12:01,410 --> 00:12:03,580
Ugh, these guys...

138
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:04,660
Hello.

139
00:12:05,310 --> 00:12:09,150
We didn't take this without permission.

140
00:12:09,150 --> 00:12:11,290
Mister Sverkel lent it to us.

141
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:13,610
What? Who?

142
00:12:13,610 --> 00:12:18,180
Mister Sverkel. He lives way down this road.

143
00:12:18,490 --> 00:12:22,890
Liar! There's no Sverkel around here.

144
00:12:22,890 --> 00:12:25,500
So you finally resorted to theft.

145
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:30,230
Huh? Does our farm have a horse like this?

146
00:12:30,230 --> 00:12:33,150
Hey, slaves. Who'd you say
you borrowed this horse from?

147
00:12:33,530 --> 00:12:38,470
I told you, Mister Sverkel!

148
00:12:38,470 --> 00:12:40,010
You don't know him?

149
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:42,620
He's a short bald old man.

150
00:12:43,170 --> 00:12:47,270
Short... bald... old man...

151
00:12:47,270 --> 00:12:50,100
Oh, the old master.

152
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,750
Oh, right. Come to think of it,
that might've been his name.

153
00:12:54,310 --> 00:12:56,630
The old master?

154
00:12:57,050 --> 00:12:59,010
You're actually lying, aren't you?

155
00:12:59,810 --> 00:13:02,250
He's a real crabby old man.

156
00:13:02,250 --> 00:13:04,370
There's no way he lent you a horse.

157
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:08,870
He's Ketil's father.

158
00:13:09,750 --> 00:13:13,330
He's the most important man on this farm.

159
00:13:25,350 --> 00:13:27,450
Looks like rain.

160
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:32,210
Thorfinn, let's stop for the day
once we finish this row.

161
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:33,510
Got it.

162
00:13:35,110 --> 00:13:37,160
Stop, stop.

163
00:13:39,290 --> 00:13:41,180
More roots.

164
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:45,060
Even though we pulled out the stumps,
the deepest roots still got left behind.

165
00:13:48,050 --> 00:13:49,400
We don't have a choice.

166
00:13:49,400 --> 00:13:51,610
This year, we'll till shallowly.

167
00:13:51,610 --> 00:13:54,720
We wouldn't want to break the plow
we borrowed by overdoing it.

168
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:01,800
It's amazing.

169
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:04,980
In one day, we've turned
that wasteland into this.

170
00:14:09,060 --> 00:14:11,160
Look. There's one over there.

171
00:14:12,310 --> 00:14:13,330
And there.

172
00:14:14,290 --> 00:14:17,040
And there. Look, they've got two.

173
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:22,340
They've got a total of ten heavy plows
after buying four more this year.

174
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:25,410
Our master is unbelievably wealthy.

175
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:28,360
Are they that expensive?

176
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:30,170
You bet they are.

177
00:14:30,170 --> 00:14:33,610
Back in my village,
eight families shared a single plow.

178
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:37,790
I'm glad the old master had one.

179
00:14:37,790 --> 00:14:41,740
The retainers definitely wouldn't
have let us borrow one.

180
00:14:42,310 --> 00:14:47,020
It must be difficult to do the
same work by hand, with hoes.

181
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:51,600
Having one of these and a horse
makes a world of difference.

182
00:14:53,700 --> 00:14:55,560
What an incredible invention...

183
00:14:55,990 --> 00:14:58,580
It must've been invented by a farmer.

184
00:14:59,330 --> 00:15:03,970
Someone unfamiliar with how difficult
farming is couldn't have made this.

185
00:15:06,070 --> 00:15:09,510
You really don't know the
first thing about farming.

186
00:15:09,510 --> 00:15:11,470
Everything's new to you.

187
00:15:11,470 --> 00:15:14,080
You're like an overgrown baby.

188
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:23,020
Aw, man. It's starting to rain.

189
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:24,390
Let's hurry, Thorfinn.

190
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:33,410
Have you prepared for the rain?

191
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:36,230
Don't change the subject.

192
00:15:36,230 --> 00:15:39,170
I'm worried about your health, Dad.

193
00:15:39,720 --> 00:15:42,360
You're getting too old to push yourself.

194
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:44,620
When you collapsed on
your farm the other day,

195
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:47,460
you got lucky because Snake
happened to be around.

196
00:15:48,550 --> 00:15:51,810
It's my wish to die on my farm.

197
00:15:52,150 --> 00:15:56,110
Living by yourself at your age
is ridiculous enough.

198
00:15:56,110 --> 00:15:58,060
You're not capable of physical labor.

199
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:01,310
Mind your own business.
I'm managing just fine.

200
00:16:01,310 --> 00:16:03,440
Don't be stubborn, Dad.

201
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:09,450
Ketil, you and I think too differently.

202
00:16:09,450 --> 00:16:12,490
If we lived together,
we'd just end up arguing again.

203
00:16:12,490 --> 00:16:14,870
Because you're so stubborn, Dad.

204
00:16:15,290 --> 00:16:18,150
We can't go in there right now.

205
00:16:21,590 --> 00:16:25,010
Think about what I said.

206
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:28,770
What are you boys doing here?

207
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:30,310
Hello.

208
00:16:30,890 --> 00:16:34,310
Dad, you can't just do whatever you want.

209
00:16:34,310 --> 00:16:36,180
These are my slaves.

210
00:16:48,150 --> 00:16:52,320
You're wet. While you're here,
wash your clothes.

211
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:57,240
Those two must not get along.

212
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:01,270
Now then, where's the washtub?

213
00:17:03,510 --> 00:17:04,670
What is it?

214
00:17:04,670 --> 00:17:07,130
It's Snake. Snake's here.

215
00:17:07,130 --> 00:17:07,920
A snake?

216
00:17:12,040 --> 00:17:14,070
What's he doing here?

217
00:17:14,070 --> 00:17:18,680
Did he sleep through that argument?

218
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:20,960
What's wrong with him?

219
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:23,060
He's sprawled out all over the place.

220
00:17:30,610 --> 00:17:33,240
Gramps, you're cooking, aren't you?

221
00:17:33,240 --> 00:17:36,230
I'm not letting you eat by yourself again.

222
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:38,160
Damn it. I see you're awake.

223
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:42,930
What are you guys doing here?
Did you come to sneak into my bed?

224
00:17:44,150 --> 00:17:45,680
Good morning.

225
00:17:47,060 --> 00:17:49,420
You're a hopeless man.

226
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:52,800
You're only awake during mealtimes.

227
00:17:53,260 --> 00:17:55,450
Don't you think that's embarrassing?

228
00:17:55,450 --> 00:18:00,650
How can a grown man like you brazenly
eat when he doesn't even work?

229
00:18:00,650 --> 00:18:02,270
Shut up, Gramps.

230
00:18:03,010 --> 00:18:05,660
Thieves come at night.

231
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:10,520
This farm is peaceful because
we patrol it every night.

232
00:18:10,520 --> 00:18:12,400
That's why I've got no problem eating.

233
00:18:13,380 --> 00:18:17,110
How are you guarding anything when all
you do is sneak into young women's homes?

234
00:18:17,110 --> 00:18:21,040
We're guarding the young women
so they don't get stolen.

235
00:18:22,070 --> 00:18:24,040
You've always got a comeback.

236
00:18:24,550 --> 00:18:26,790
So do you, Gramps.

237
00:18:29,330 --> 00:18:31,450
What are you guys doing?

238
00:18:31,450 --> 00:18:34,380
Come over here and eat.
You haven't eaten yet, right?

239
00:18:37,670 --> 00:18:39,140
Are you sure?

240
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,440
Eat, eat. The food ain't so fancy
that you need to be shy.

241
00:18:42,440 --> 00:18:43,640
How dare you.

242
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:48,900
Have a seat. If you worked today,
then you've earned the right to eat.

243
00:18:53,510 --> 00:18:56,910
Is it true that thieves come to the farm?

244
00:18:57,350 --> 00:18:59,720
They do. All the time.

245
00:18:59,720 --> 00:19:03,590
They come for unharvested wheat,
pigs, that kind of thing.

246
00:19:03,590 --> 00:19:05,040
Huh.

247
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:07,800
Some of them are armed, too.

248
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:12,250
Beating up those guys
and capturing them is our job.

249
00:19:12,250 --> 00:19:15,180
We really do work around here.

250
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:21,630
What we'd lose to thieves
is equal to what we pay you.

251
00:19:21,630 --> 00:19:25,440
You really know
how to piss someone off, don't you?

252
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:29,690
We could chase off thieves without your help.

253
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:32,440
We're Nordic men.

254
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:35,030
We know how to use swords and spears.

255
00:19:35,790 --> 00:19:39,320
Oh? You're saying you can
still fight at your age?

256
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:41,740
Don't flatter yourself, Snake.

257
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:45,150
There are plenty of fighters
more skilled than you.

258
00:19:45,150 --> 00:19:48,330
I'm just worried about your health, old man.

259
00:19:48,330 --> 00:19:51,880
I'm not so decrepit that I need your concern.

260
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:53,100
Don't be so angry.

261
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:55,590
You'll collapse again, Grandpa.

262
00:19:56,630 --> 00:20:03,130
In reality, despite what you say, this farm's
fighting force is too small for its scale.

263
00:20:03,130 --> 00:20:06,980
The 13 men I've got have
their hands full chasing down thieves.

264
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:13,480
You could easily take over
this farm with 50 to 60 men.

265
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:15,620
Right, Thorfinn?

266
00:20:15,620 --> 00:20:16,240
Huh?

267
00:20:19,670 --> 00:20:22,740
Oh, um... I'm not sure.

268
00:20:26,130 --> 00:20:30,500
Nothing tougher than thieves
ever comes to this place.

269
00:20:30,830 --> 00:20:32,680
You don't know that.

270
00:20:32,680 --> 00:20:35,190
It'll be too late once
it's already happening.

271
00:20:35,190 --> 00:20:36,510
Don't worry.

272
00:20:36,510 --> 00:20:39,880
Regardless of what happens,
I won't ever come crying to your lot.

273
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:44,640
Why don't you get anything
tougher than thieves here?

274
00:20:46,810 --> 00:20:48,890
S-Sorry.

275
00:20:50,020 --> 00:20:52,270
Because of King Harald's influence.

276
00:20:52,930 --> 00:20:59,420
Twice a year, Ketil sends an absurd
amount of tribute to him.

277
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:04,680
Because the king accepts his tribute,
he's obligated to protect Ketil.

278
00:21:07,250 --> 00:21:09,290
It's foolishness.

279
00:21:09,290 --> 00:21:12,350
Buying peace with money is foolishness.

280
00:21:12,350 --> 00:21:18,800
What need is there to hold so much land
a single farmer can't defend it himself?

281
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:23,870
How many days do you think it
takes to get here from Jelling?

282
00:21:23,870 --> 00:21:27,430
We're the ones actually defending the farm.

283
00:21:27,750 --> 00:21:30,920
Too much wealth brings about misfortune.

284
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:32,270
You're ignoring me now?

285
00:21:32,270 --> 00:21:35,630
Increasing your wealth
makes you fear losing it.

286
00:21:35,630 --> 00:21:40,030
You spend money to assuage your fears
and then have to earn more.

287
00:21:40,480 --> 00:21:44,780
And my idiot son hasn't realized yet
how pointless it is.

288
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:52,920
I've had enough.
Old men love giving lectures.

289
00:21:55,920 --> 00:21:58,440
Hey, it stopped raining.

290
00:22:03,640 --> 00:22:06,870
Now then, it's time
to go out patrolling again.

291
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:08,140
Don't come back here.

292
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:17,190
But you miss me when I don't.

293
00:22:17,190 --> 00:22:18,760
Get out of here!

294
00:22:26,280 --> 00:22:28,360
Those two were weird.

295
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:31,510
They get along better than
the actual father and son.

296
00:22:31,510 --> 00:22:32,230
Yeah.

297
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:38,420
I feel like I learned a lot of things today.

298
00:22:41,270 --> 00:22:44,300
Good for you, you overgrown baby.

299
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:45,680
Don't call me that.

300
00:22:56,440 --> 00:22:59,080
Too much wealth, huh?

301
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:19,230
You can spread more seed than that.

302
00:23:19,230 --> 00:23:19,840
What?

303
00:23:20,660 --> 00:23:22,590
But isn't this all we have?

304
00:23:22,970 --> 00:23:26,340
It'll be fine. Grab huge handfuls.

305
00:23:26,870 --> 00:23:28,970
I'm your friend. Trust me.

306
00:23:29,460 --> 00:23:30,510
Friend?

307
00:23:30,510 --> 00:23:32,480
What? No way.

308
00:23:33,100 --> 00:23:35,180
Why would you question that?

309
00:23:35,180 --> 00:23:36,520
We're friends, aren't we?

310
00:23:36,830 --> 00:23:38,790
What? We're not?

311
00:23:49,110 --> 00:23:50,000
No...

312
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:55,640
We're friends.

313
00:25:36,470 --> 00:25:39,980
Iron Fist Ketil

314
00:25:37,770 --> 00:25:39,480
"Iron Fist Ketil."
